## 5. System Design

### 5.1 Data Storage

Instead of using a single class/object per book, the system uses four parallel `ArrayList`s, where the same index across all four lists represents one book record:

- `ids` – stores the Book ID (`Integer`)
- `titles` – stores the Book Title (`String`)
- `Authors` – stores the Author Name (`String`)
- `issued` – stores the issue status (`Boolean`)

### 5.2 Modules / Functions

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `main()` | Displays the menu in a loop and routes user choice to the correct function |
| `addBook()` | Takes book details as input and adds them to the lists |
| `viewBooks()` | Iterates through all lists and prints book details and status |
| `searchBook()` | Searches for books whose title contains the search string |
| `issueBook()` | Finds a book by ID and marks it as issued, if available |
| `returnBook()` | Finds a book by ID and marks it as returned |
| `deleteBook()` | Finds a book by ID and removes its entry from all four lists |

### 5.3 Program Flow

1. The program starts and displays a menu in an infinite loop.
2. The user enters a choice (1–7).
3. Based on the choice, the corresponding function is called.
4. The function performs the operation and prints a result/status message.
5. Control returns to the menu until the user selects option 7 to exit.

## 6. Implementation Details

- User input is read using `Scanner`, with `sc.nextInt()` used for numeric input (IDs, menu choice) and `sc.nextLine()` used for text input (titles, author names).
- After every `sc.nextInt()`, an extra `sc.nextLine()` call is used to clear the leftover newline character from the input buffer, preventing input-skipping bugs.
- Searching is implemented using `String.contains()`, making it a substring match rather than an exact match.
- Issue/Return/Delete operations all work by looping through the `ids` list to find a matching index, then acting on that index across the other lists.

