## 8. Known Limitations

- Data is not saved between program runs (no file or database persistence).
- Search functionality is case-sensitive.
- No validation is performed to prevent duplicate Book IDs.
- No confirmation prompt before deleting a book.
- Minimal error handling for invalid/non-numeric input.

## 9. Future Enhancements

- Add file-based or database persistence (e.g., using file I/O or JDBC with MySQL/SQLite).
- Make search case-insensitive and support search by author or ID as well.
- Add input validation and duplicate ID checks.
- Introduce a proper `Book` class instead of parallel ArrayLists, for cleaner and more scalable code.
- Build a GUI (JavaFX/Swing) or web-based front end.

## 10. Conclusion

This project successfully demonstrates a working library management system using core Java concepts. While simple in scope, it lays the groundwork for understanding how basic CRUD operations, collections, and control flow come together to build a functional application. It also highlights several areas that can be improved to make it more robust, scalable, and production-ready.
